BlackHat 0.9.9 (CMS's tree, cms-externals/blackhat cms/v0.9.9) on GCC 15 and today's autotools (2026-10-23):
- configure.ac: automake 1.19 (the base's) warns that the libtool libraries need AM_PROG_AR, and -Werror made that fatal,
  so `autoreconf -fi` (the recipe's pre) failed. The tarball's own libtool cannot be kept: it misreads the
  conda compiler's link line (a bare -L before crtendS.o: "undefined reference to __TMC_END__").
- src/cached_OLHA.cpp: one branch returned the object where the function returns a pointer; GCC 15 refuses
  it. The other three branches allocate with new, as this one now does (CMS's spec seds the same line).
- src/tree1.cc, src/partial_amplitude.cpp: GenerateMomentum<T> and partial_amplitude_cached::eval_fn<T> are
  defined in one file and called from others with the declaration alone; GCC 15 inlines the defining file's
  own calls and emits no copy, so libBG and libassembly were left with undefined symbols (seen when utils/
  links them). Explicit instantiations, for the precisions the libraries call: R, RHP (and RVHP).
- my_programs/Makefile.am: `include $(DEPDIR)/*.Po` needs a .deps/ that today's automake no longer makes
  beforehand ("No rule to make target '.deps/*.Po'"); -include.
- src/Interface/BH_interface.cpp, src/settings.cpp: functions declared to return a value that never return
  one. GCC (15 here) takes the end of such a function as unreachable and emits no return, so a call runs on into
  whatever code follows: BH_interface::set, the first thing Sherpa calls, crashed (SIGSEGV), and
  cached_BH_Ampl::operator() (every cached amplitude) returned its result only by register accident. The
  four set()s return true, cached_BH_Ampl what it computed, the two stand-in amplitudes 1. (as their
  siblings), print_setting true. (The build's -Wreturn-type warnings name them, "no return statement";
  those left are error stubs, "Not yet implemented", and switches over every valid case.)
- src/color_algebra.cpp: operator< of color_string fell off the end when the fundamental labels were equal
  and the adjoint ones differed (undefined: any answer, so a std::map keyed on it could lose entries). Now
  lexicographic, fundamental labels then adjoint, the same answer wherever it gave one.
